Q: How can the RecyclerView&#39;s Observer return the current filtered list after item delete? A: <p>You do have the right idea: the UI observes <strong>one</strong> <code>LiveData</code> (<code>cardsMediatorList</code>) and the <code>ViewModel</code> swaps its source depending on the selected filter/sort.</p>
<p>What you’re looking at is basically:</p>
<p>When a filtered list is visible and you delete an item, Room emits a new value, but the UI ends up observing the full <em>list</em> again</p>
<p>Room itself is not “ignoring” your filter. It simply re-emits whatever query the current <code>LiveData</code> represents. If the UI suddenly gets the full list, it means the <code>MediatorLiveData</code> switched back to the <code>getAllCards()</code> source at some point</p>
<p>And there’s one very likely reason in the code you posted. Your filtered DAO method requires a parameter:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Query(&quot;SELECT * FROM cards_table WHERE cardType = :cardType&quot;)
LiveData&lt;List&lt;Card&gt;&gt; getFiltRedCards(String cardType);
</code></pre>
<p>But in the repository you call it without one:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>case DB_FILTRED:
    return cardDao.getFiltRedCards(); // ← missing argument
</code></pre>
<p>Either the real code passes different values over time, or the filter state isn’t preserved when the source is recreated which all result in the filter state not being part of the observable stream</p>
<p>Instead of manually swapping sources, make the filter itself <code>LiveData</code> and derive the list from it by using switchMap:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class CardViewModel extends AndroidViewModel {

    private final CardRepository repository;

    private final MutableLiveData&lt;CardRepository.SortOrder&gt; sortOrder =
            new MutableLiveData&lt;&gt;(CardRepository.SortOrder.DB_SORTORDER);

    private final LiveData&lt;List&lt;Card&gt;&gt; cards =
            Transformations.switchMap(sortOrder, order -&gt;
                    repository.getSortedItems(order)
            );

    public CardViewModel(@NonNull Application application) {
        super(application);
        repository = new CardRepository(application);
    }

    public LiveData&lt;List&lt;Card&gt;&gt; getCards() {
        return cards;
    }

    public void setSortOrder(CardRepository.SortOrder newOrder) {
        sortOrder.setValue(newOrder);
    }

    public void delete(Card card) {
        repository.delete(card);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>No <code>MediatorLiveData</code> &amp; no manual source swapping.</p>
<p>The filter becomes part of the reactive chain.</p>
<p>Make sure the filtered query actually uses the filter value though:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public LiveData&lt;List&lt;Card&gt;&gt; getSortedItems(SortOrder order) {
    switch (order) {
        case DB_FILTRED:
            return cardDao.getFiltRedCards(&quot;RED&quot;);
        case DB_SORTORDER:
        default:
            return cardDao.getAllCards();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to deal with datepicker in Appium Android A: <p>I had a similar issue with interacting with a <code>DatePicker</code> in Appium Android, and this is the solution that worked for me.</p>
<p>The key points to note are:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Locating the NumberPicker Fields:</strong> I used the XPath to locate the <code>EditText</code> fields inside the <code>NumberPicker</code> widget:<br />
<code>(//android.widget.EditText[@resource-id='android:id/numberpicker_input'])</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Clearing and Setting Values:</strong> Each field needed to be cleared before entering the new text. Without clearing, the input wouldn't be properly set.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Tapping Out of the Field:</strong> After entering text into a field, I had to tap out of the column (e.g., tapping another field) to ensure the value was properly set.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here’s the code snippet that worked for me:</p>
<pre><code>// XPath to locate the NumberPicker EditText fields  
string rowPickerXPath = &quot;(//android.widget.EditText[@resource-id='android:id/numberpicker_input'])&quot;;  

// Locate each column (month, day, year) using their index  
IWebElement monthField = driver.FindElement(By.XPath(rowPickerXPath + &quot;[1]&quot;));  
IWebElement dayField = driver.FindElement(By.XPath(rowPickerXPath + &quot;[2]&quot;));  
IWebElement yearField = driver.FindElement(By.XPath(rowPickerXPath + &quot;[3]&quot;));  

// Clear and set the month value  
monthField.Clear();  
monthField.SendKeys(monthAbbrevName); // Example: &quot;Jul&quot;  
monthField.Click(); // Tap out of the field  

// Clear and set the day value  
dayField.Clear();  
dayField.SendKeys(day); // Example: &quot;15&quot;  
dayField.Click(); // Tap out of the field  

// Clear and set the year value  
yearField.Clear();  
yearField.SendKeys(year); // Example: &quot;2023&quot;  
yearField.Click(); // Tap out of the field  

// Tap the day field again to finalize the selection  
dayField.Click();  
</code></pre>
<h3>Key Points:</h3>
<ul>
<li><p>The <code>Clear()</code> method is used to erase the existing text in the <code>EditText</code> field before entering new values.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The <code>SendKeys()</code> method sets the text value for each field.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The <code>Click()</code> method ensures the field value is applied by tapping out of the column.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>This approach worked for me because the <code>DatePicker</code> required explicit clearing of fields and tapping out to finalize the input. If you're facing a similar issue, make sure to inspect the widget and confirm the behavior of its <code>EditText</code> components.</p>
